#
# hpc RCU options: which liburcu flavour <hpc/rcu.h> binds to.
#
# In a file of its own for the reason the memory options are (Kconfig.mem): a
# tree that embeds this package sources the same definitions instead of keeping
# a copy of them in step by hand. hpc's own Kconfig sources it where the block
# used to be, so nothing about a standalone hpc build changes.
#
#
# CONFIG_THREADS and CONFIG_RCU themselves are declared once, in the shared
# scripts/Kconfig.compiler (kbuild submodule), so hpc, crypto and un see the
# same symbols. CONFIG_RCU turns on the lockless variants of the intrusive
# containers - <hpc/queue.h>, <hpc/rbtree.h>, <hpc/hash/table.h> - which bind to
# the vendored liburcu (vendor/userspace-rcu) through <hpc/rcu.h>. What is
# hpc-specific, and lives here, is which liburcu flavour that binding uses:
# the choice below picks both the header and the archive that gets linked, so
# readers can never end up paired with another flavour's grace periods.
#

choice
	prompt "RCU flavour (liburcu)"
	depends on RCU
	default RCU_MEMB
	help
	  Which liburcu flavour <hpc/rcu.h> binds to. It selects the read-side
	  cost and what a read-side thread owes the implementation; the vendored
	  liburcu is built and linked to match.

config RCU_MEMB
	bool "memb - sys_membarrier (default)"
	help
	  Readers do plain loads and stores, writers force the barrier on every
	  other thread with sys_membarrier(). The best default: a read side
	  nearly as cheap as QSBR with none of its obligations. Each thread
	  entering a read-side section must call rcu_register_thread() first.

	  Falls back to a signal-based scheme where the kernel has no
	  membarrier support.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config RCU_QSBR
	bool "qsbr - quiescent-state based"
	help
	  The cheapest read side available - read-side sections cost nothing at
	  all - in exchange for an obligation: nothing marks where a reader is
	  done, so every read-side thread must announce a quiescent state
	  itself, with rcu_quiescent_state(), often enough that writers make
	  progress. For hot loops that can be instrumented that way.

config RCU_BP
	bool "bp - bulletproof"
	help
	  Registers a thread on its first read-side section and needs no
	  unregistration, at the price of a slower read side. For code that is
	  loaded into a process it does not own (a library, a plugin) and so
	  cannot make every thread register.

endchoice
